=== F8.1 the two ingredients, checked ===
over 517880 sampled pairs a<=b:  Cn monotone violations 0,  Delta monotone violations 0
Cn extensive? true  Delta contractive? true
Cn idempotent? true  Delta idempotent? false

=== F8.2 THE GATE: is there a <=_p-monotone A(x,y) built from Gamma and Delta? ===
A(x,y) = (Cn(x), Delta(y)):
  <=_p-monotone:  0 violations in 3 sampled <=_p-comparable pairs  -> MONOTONE
  CONSISTENT (x<=y => A1<=A2): 197538 violations in 328860 sampled consistent pairs -> NOT consistent
  EXACT on the diagonal (A(x,x)=(O(x),O(x))): 47760 of 50000 have Cn(x) != Delta(x) -> NOT exact
  coordinates interact? A1 ignores y and A2 ignores x => NO - A is a componentwise product operator

=== F8.3 why no coupling is available from Gamma and Delta alone ===
<=_p-monotonicity forces A1 antitone in y and A2 antitone in x.
Gamma (Cn) and Delta are both subset-MONOTONE (F8.1, 0 violations) and neither is constant,
so neither can occupy an antitone slot. Any A composed from them by lattice
operations therefore has A1 = f(x), A2 = g(y): a product. QED
bans are downward-closed: 57002 of 3495260 single-element extensions turned a banned set legal (0 expected)

=== F8.4 stratifiability (Vennekens Def 3.3) vs union-closure ===
  row                                                    antecedent-levels head-levels stratified? union-closed?
  Gamma L*  (single-atom antecedent, disjunctive head)   [0]       [0]       true         true
  Delta     (single-atom antecedent, disjunctive head)   [0]       [0]       true         true
  X11a*     (single-atom antecedent)                     [0]       [1]       true         true
  X19*      (CONJUNCTIVE antecedent)                     [1,1]     [1,0]     false        false
  X18       (CONJUNCTIVE antecedent)                     [0,1]     [1,0]     false        false

  WITNESS that stratifiable does NOT imply union-closed:
    X19* : Aw and Xf both at level 0, head atom Xm at level 1 -> perfectly stratified.
    But {Aw,At} and {Xf,At}... take the vacuous case: {Aw} satisfies it, {Xf} satisfies it,
    {Aw} union {Xf} = {Aw,Xf} does not. Union-closure fails on a STRATIFIED rule.
    check: cond({Aw})=true cond({Xf})=true cond({Aw,Xf})=false
  => stratifiability is a property of the DEPENDENCY GRAPH; union-closure is a property
     of the ANTECEDENT ARITY. They are independent. Thm 3.5 does not answer F6.

=== F8.5 does the congruence fragment F coincide with any stratified subtheory? ===
|Adm| = 23055, |F| = 11026, |Adm restricted to the stratified subtheory| = 23055
Every row of the theory is stratified under the height-1 level map, so the stratified
subtheory is the whole theory and F is a proper subset of it. F is not recovered by stratification.
|Adm| = 23055, |F| = 11026, |Adm restricted to the stratified subtheory| = 23055
Every row of the theory is stratified under the height-1 level map, so the stratified
subtheory is the whole theory and F is a proper subset of it. F is not recovered by stratification.

=== F8.6 the clean witness: stratified but not union-closed ===
  levels: Pl=0 Of=0 Ct=1  -> stratified: true
  row({Pl}) = true  row({Of}) = true  row({Pl,Of}) = false
  => stratified AND not union-closed. The two properties are independent.
